## 3. Service Interface Pattern

### Interface-Based Design

**Service Interfaces** define contracts that services must implement, enabling loose coupling and testability.

### MaritimeIQ Example

```csharp
// Interface
public interface IMaritimeIntelligenceService
{
    Task<MaritimeIntelligenceDashboard> GetIntelligenceDashboardAsync();
    Task<VesselPatternAnalysis> AnalyzeVesselPatternsAsync(
        string vesselId, DateTime startDate, DateTime endDate);
    Task<PredictiveInsights> GetPredictiveInsightsAsync(string category);
}

// Implementation
public class MaritimeIntelligenceService : BaseMaritimeService, IMaritimeIntelligenceService
{
    public override string ServiceName => "Maritime Intelligence Service";

    public MaritimeIntelligenceService(
        ILogger<MaritimeIntelligenceService> logger, 
        IConfiguration? configuration = null) 
        : base(logger, configuration)
    {
    }

    public async Task<MaritimeIntelligenceDashboard> GetIntelligenceDashboardAsync()
    {
        return await ExecuteOperationAsync(async () =>
        {
            LogInformation("Generating maritime intelligence dashboard");
            
            // Business logic here
            await Task.Delay(200);
            
            return new MaritimeIntelligenceDashboard
            {
                Widgets = new List<IntelligenceWidget>
                {
                    // Dashboard data
                }
            };
        }, nameof(GetIntelligenceDashboardAsync));
    }
}
```

**Benefits**:
- Loose coupling (depend on interfaces, not implementations)
- Testability (easy to mock)
- Multiple implementations possible
- Clear contracts

